{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's death in 1152 Raymond II was succeeded by Raymond III who was then a minor. \n\n*Early Life and Regency* \nRaymond's early life was marked by the regency of his mother Hodierna and her sister Melisende the Queen of Jerusalem. In 1155 Raymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi the ruler of Aleppo and Damascus. He was released in 1156 or 1157 after a ransom was paid and an agreement was made to release some Muslim prisoners. \n\nAfter his release Raymond took part in the Battle of Lake Huleh in 1157 where he was defeated by Nur ad-Din. In 1160 or 1161 Raymond was again taken captive by Nur ad-Din and was released in 1164 or 1173 after a long period of captivity. During his captivity the County of Tripoli was governed by various regents including Amalric the Count of Jaffa and Ascalon.\n\n*Reign and Military Campaigns* \nAfter his release from captivity Raymond played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the Haute Cour and was a strong opponent of the ruling king Guy of Lusignan. In 1180 Raymond married Eschiva of Bures the widow of Walter of Saint Omer and Princess of Galilee. \n\nIn 1187 Raymond was involved in a dispute with Raynald of Châtillon a fellow Crusader leader. Raynald had attacked a Muslim caravan and Raymond had negotiated a truce with Saladin the ruler of Egypt and Syria. The dispute between Raymond and Raynald contributed to the division among the Crusader leaders and ultimately led to the Battle of Hattin.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death* \nThe Battle of Hattin was a decisive defeat for the Crusaders and marked a turning point in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond played a key role in the battle but was ultimately unable to prevent the defeat. After the battle Raymond fled to Tyre where he died soon after. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond's death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Crusader states in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.011065074242651463}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0003358753747306764}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0016014858847483993}, {"token": " nob", "logprob": -0.19904756546020508}, {"token": "leman", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.019826099276542664}, {"token": " leader", "logprob": -0.9182716608047485}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.06198464706540108}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.10050278156995773}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.6948085427284241}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.3869574964046478}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " state", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.08102524280548096}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.004425494000315666}, {"token": " Holy", "logprob": -0.12713634967803955}, {"token": " Land", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.019608115777373314}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.0015778008382767439}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0006531727267429233}, {"token": " born", "logprob": -0.018500978127121925}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.9740880727767944}, {"